      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Headings": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "There are 6 heading variants. The number of \"#\" symbols, followed by text, indicates the importance of the heading.": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Heading 1": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Heading 2": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Heading 3": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Heading 4": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Heading 5": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Heading 6": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Emphasis": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Modifying text is so neat and easy. You can make your text bold, italic and strikethrough.": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Using two asterisks **this text is bold**.  \nTwo underscores **work as well**.  \nLet's make it *italic now*.  \nYou guessed it, *one underscore is also enough*.  \nCan we combine ***both of that*?** Absolutely.  \nWhat if I want to ~~strikethrough~~?": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Blockquote": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Want to emphasise importance of the text? Say no more.": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "This is a blockquote.\nWant to write on a new line with space between?": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Images": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "The best way is to simply drag & drop image from your computer directly. You can also create reference to image and assign it that way.  \nHere is the syntax.": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "text if the image fails to load": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Links": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Similar to images, links can also be inserted directly or by creating a reference. You can create both inline and block links.": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Like it so far? Follow me on GitHub  \nMy Markdown Cheatsheet - star it if you like it  \nFind some great docs here": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Code": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "You can cerate both inline and full block code snippets. You can also define programming language you were using in your snippet. All by using backticks.": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "I created `.env` file at the root.\nBackticks inside backticks? ``No problem.``": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Lists": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "As you can do in HTML, Markdown allows creating of both ordered and unordered lists.": {
    "msgstr": "",
    "references": [
      "src/data/pages/en/markdown.md"
    ]
  },
  "Ordered List": {
